# V.32bis at 14400 bit/s -- the top of the Recommendation, and the only
# fixture here that nobody at this end recorded.
#
# A Banksia MyModem 144 (Rockwell) calling out, published with the audio
# by Gough Lui and split into one file per direction; this is the
# answering modem's half, so modec places the call.  Nothing in it came
# out of this code, which is the point: a fixture minted from our own
# recording can pass by agreeing with a mistake we make twice, and this
# one cannot.
#
# There is no text.  The two modems trained, connected and idled, so the
# reference is empty and stays empty -- which is an assertion in its own
# right at 14400, where a receiver that has lost the line hands the
# terminal a page of noise rather than nothing.
#
# What this fixture is really for is the retrain count.  Before the
# timing loop was given a tracking gain of its own, this same recording
# connected at 14400 -- the negotiation was never the problem -- and
# then asked for a retrain four seconds later, and again ten seconds
# after that, with the decision error sitting at 0.068 against a margin
# of 0.11.  It now runs at 0.040 and asks for nothing.  Connecting is
# what 'connect:' pins; holding the line is what 'retrains:' pins, and
# only the second of them ever moved.
source:    recordings/third-party/banksia-mymodem144-answer.wav
seconds:   32.0
role:      originate
modes:     v32bis
v8:        no
connect:   V32bis 14400
retrains:  0
tolerance: 0
